  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong to severe thunderstorms possible this\nafternoon/evening.\n\nA westward retrograding upper level trough over the northeast\nwill push back the upper level ridge that has maintained itself\nover the region over the last couple of days. As this occurs\ntoday, the amount of subsidence across the area should decrease\nas the upper level ridge retreats west. This will allow storms \nto also track through the region from the northwest from pieces \nof shortwave energy track southeast along the eastern periphery \nof the aforementioned upper level ridge. Along with these pieces\nof upper level shortwave propagating southeast along the mean\nflow, a backdoor cold front looks to sink southwest from the\nnortheast thats in association with the aforementioned upper\nlevel trough. This cold front will aid in the forcing for storms\nacross the area during the afternoon and evening hours today. \n\nThe environment today supports strong damaging wind gusts once\nagain across the region with 1500-2000 J/Kg of SBCAPE, and \n1000-1400 J/Kg of DCAPE. Any storms that develop in this \nenvironment will likely produce strong damaging outflows through\nthe late afternoon and evening hours. The biggest difference\ntoday is that deep layer shear looks to be slightly higher\nacross the region today, with 20-30 knots of 0-6km deep layer\nshear forecast today. This will lead to multicellular storm\nclusters developing across the area that will likely travel to\nthe south and east through the afternoon and evening. \n\nBy Sunday, model guidance brings the frontal boundary south of\nthe area into southern North Carolina. This will put an end to\nmost storm activity across the region; however, low level\neasterly flow combined with mid level shortwave disturbances\nlook to lead to some isolated showers along and west of the Blue\nRidge Sunday afternoon and evening. \n\nThe remainder of the week looks to stay dry across the region as\nthe upper level ridge slides back east on Monday. This will\nbring a return of hot temperatures to the region through the\nmiddle of next week before a frontal passage on Friday looks to\nbreak down the upper level ridge across the eastern Conus.",
